            <ArticleTitle>Self-reported physical activity and myocardial flow reserve in postmenopausal women at risk for cardiovascular disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>45-50</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Regular exercise protects against coronary heart disease (CHD) events and improves vascular reactivity. Exercise effects on myocardial flow reserve (MFR) are not well studied.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We performed dynamic N-13 ammonia positron emission tomography (PET) in 16 postmenopausal women (60 +/- 6 years) to measure myocardial blood flow (MBF) and MFR. We also obtained information from each woman on her self-reported physical activity.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Of the 16 patients, 6 reported moderate regular physical activity, and 10 did not. Women who reported regular, at least moderate physical activity had a higher percentage increase in adenosine MBF from rest compared with women who did not exercise (268% vs. 129%, p = 0.04) and had a significantly higher mean maximal MFR (3.68 vs. 2.29, p = 0.04).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">These findings provide further mechanistic support for the beneficial cardiovascular effects of exercise.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
